// loft#825 — reusing a `for` variable over a second vector with a different element type
// has a diagnostic that names the loop variable and prescribes a cure that works. It has to
// arrive FIRST, ahead of whatever the body then fails on.
//
// The body error below is a CONSEQUENCE of the conflict: in the second loop `p` still
// carries the first loop's element type, so `p.0` is a float and the integer accumulator is
// what trips. Reported alone it names `hs`, a variable that is not the problem, and tells
// the reader to rename it — which reproduces the same error under the new name, forever.
// Renaming `p` is the only fix, and the first line is where it is said.
//
// A `for` variable can only conflict where the body USES it, so a body error is the common
// case, not the exotic one; the print-only shape that already reported this correctly is
// the rarer one. Both orderings are pinned here.

fn main() {
  a: vector<(float, float, float)> = [(1.0, 2.0, 3.0)];
  s = 0.0;
  for p in a { s = s + p.0; }

  hb: vector<(integer, text)> = [(11, "x"), (44, "y")];
  hs = 0;
  for p in hb { hs = hs + p.0; }

  println("s={s} hs={hs}");
}
